<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mafra National Palace</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1717">1717</a>–<a href="/years/1755">1755</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Palace of Mafra, Palace-Convent of Mafra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mafra National Palace is a huge baroque palace, basilica and Franciscan friary at Mafra, north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, built for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> V from 1717 and paid for with Brazilian gold. Designed by João Frederico Ludovice, it is one of the largest palaces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and includes a famous library and two carillons. It was listed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2019.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mafra-national-palace.jpg" alt="Mafra National Palace" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pedro S Bello · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Europa_-_Portugal_-_Lisboa_-_Mafra_-_Palacio_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>King John V of Portugal made a vow in 1711 to build a convent if his wife, Maria Anna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, bore him a child, and the birth of his first daughter, the Infanta Barbara, led to construction. The site was chosen in 1713 and bought in 1716, and the first stone was laid on 17 November 1717 before the king, his court and the Cardinal Patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>. It began as a small friary for 13 Capuchin friars who were to live in strict poverty. When gold and diamonds from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> arrived in Lisbon in abundance, the king announced a sumptuous palace and a much larger friary instead.</p><p>He made João Frederico Ludovice director of the royal works. Several other architects took part, including Carlos Baptista Garbo, Custódio Vieira and Manuel da Maia, but one style runs through the whole building. Construction lasted 13 years and employed on average 15,000 workers daily, up to 45,000 at the peak, with 7,000 soldiers keeping order. A total of 1,383 workers died. The basilica and convent were inaugurated on 22 October 1730, the king's 41st birthday, with eight days of festivities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The facade is 220 metres long, with a square domed tower at each end and the white marble basilica at the centre, flanked by the royal palace. John V had sought advice from his ambassador in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and the central balcony echoes that of St Peter's Basilica. The towers and the dome were inspired by Sant'Agnese in Agone by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-borromini">Francesco Borromini</a>. The complex has about 1,200 rooms and housed up to 330 friars. The basilica has 58 marble statues by Roman sculptors, six organs built between 1792 and 1807, and carillons cast in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>. The Rococo library, 88 metres long, houses bats that protect the books from insects.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The palace was never lived in permanently, since the royal family found it gloomy, but it served for hunting. Work stopped in 1755, when workers were needed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> after the earthquake. The royal family fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> in 1807, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-andoche-junot">Jean-Andoche Junot</a>, driven out by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a>, used the palace. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> left in 1834, and the monastery was given to the military in 1849. The last king, Manuel II, left from Mafra on 5 October 1910 for exile. A Mafra School of Sculpture, headed by Alessandro Giusti, trained Portuguese sculptors, among them Joaquim Machado de Castro. José Saramago's novel Baltasar and Blimunda describes its building. It was inscribed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> on 7 July 2019.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Palace of Mafra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
